  "id": "DrugBank_DB13365 - MONDO_1567",
  "verbalization": "Calcium Lactate Gluconate is associated with Nephrocalcinosis through a pathway involving Phosphorus Metabolism Disease, as indicated by three distinct but overlapping evidence chains. Each of these paths links Calcium Lactate Gluconate to a phosphorus-related disorder, which in turn is linked to Nephrocalcinosis. The shared involvement of phosphorus metabolism suggests a potential mechanism where dysregulated phosphate levels contribute to kidney calcium deposition. The recurring association with Phosphorus Metabolism Disease implies that the drug may influence metabolic processes that are central to the development of nephrolithiasis. While the exact mechanism remains unclear, the consistent pathway highlights a possible role in modulating phosphate homeostasis, which could lead to the formation of calcium deposits in the kidneys. The evidence supports a plausible link between the drug and Nephrocalcinosis, though further research is needed to elucidate the precise biological pathways involved.",
